WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.240
If you're learning a language, practicing with 
native speakers is the fastest way to become fluent.

00:00:04.240 --> 00:00:07.080
Take effective online lessons 
with native teachers on italki.

00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:10.040
Buy your first lesson and get your second lesson free. 
Link in description.

00:00:19.660 --> 00:00:23.060
Hello everyone. Welcome to the Langfocus channel 
and my name is Paul.

00:00:23.060 --> 00:00:25.840
Today I'm going to talk about the Kurdish language.

00:00:26.180 --> 00:00:27.460
Where is Kurdish spoken?

00:00:27.640 --> 00:00:28.940
Well, that's an interesting question.

00:00:28.960 --> 00:00:34.060
Kurdish is the language of the Kurdish people, an ethnic 
group who do not have their own independent country.

00:00:34.140 --> 00:00:35.940
They mostly live in the Kurdistan region,

00:00:36.160 --> 00:00:41.820
which consists of much of southeast Turkey, northeastern Syria, northern Iraq, northwestern Iran,

00:00:41.980 --> 00:00:44.860
as well as some other isolated areas 
in the former Soviet Union.

00:00:44.960 --> 00:00:47.620
Kurdish is the majority language spoken in those areas.

00:00:47.780 --> 00:00:51.300
There are approximately 30 million 
native speakers of Kurdish.

00:00:51.580 --> 00:00:59.680
That includes around 14.5 million in Turkey, 
5.6 million in Iraq, 6 million in Iran 
and around 2 million in Syria.

00:01:00.140 --> 00:01:05.100
It also includes a Kurdish diaspora living outside 
of the Kurdistan Region, most notably in Germany.

00:01:05.220 --> 00:01:10.060
Like Persian, Kurdish is a member of the Indo-Iranian
branch of the Indo-European language family.

00:01:10.180 --> 00:01:14.900
But whereas Persian belongs to the southwestern sub-branch, 
Kurdish belongs to the northwestern sub-branch.

00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:18.660
Persian and Kurdish share many similarities 
but they're not mutually intelligible.

00:01:18.840 --> 00:01:22.220
The history of the Kurdish language 
is the subject of much speculation.

00:01:22.300 --> 00:01:25.420
For much of its history, the Kurdish language 
was not a written language.

00:01:25.640 --> 00:01:29.340
Folk tales and poetry were passed down orally 
from generation to generation.

00:01:29.440 --> 00:01:34.420
With few exceptions, the earliest written Kurdish texts 
come from the 15th century and later.

00:01:34.600 --> 00:01:38.540
There was one known poem from the seventh century,
written in the Gorani dialect.

00:01:39.200 --> 00:01:43.220
But technically, the Gorani dialect is actually 
a separate language from Kurdish.

00:01:43.540 --> 00:01:46.320
Okay, wait! This is getting confusing. 
Let me back up for a minute.

00:01:46.540 --> 00:01:51.520
What is the Kurdish language? Is it one unified language 
that all Kurdish people can understand and speak?

00:01:51.680 --> 00:01:52.800
No, not really.

00:01:53.040 --> 00:02:00.040
It's a collection of related dialect spread out 
across a continuum, meaning that the language 
changes gradually over a geographic distance.

00:02:00.360 --> 00:02:05.100
There are three main dialect groups of Kurdish: 
Kurmanji, Sorani and Pehlewani

00:02:05.480 --> 00:02:09.400
The most widely spoken is Kurmanji 
with around 20 million native speakers.

00:02:09.640 --> 00:02:15.140
It is the major dialect spoken in Turkey as well as 
in Syria and some parts of Iraq and parts of Iran.

00:02:15.920 --> 00:02:22.080
The second most widely spoken is Sorani, with 
6 or 7 million native speakers mainly in Iraq and Iran.

00:02:23.140 --> 00:02:31.320
The third most widely spoken is Pehlewani,
which is spoken by about 3 million people in some
Kurdish areas in Iran and one district in Iraqi Kurdistan

00:02:32.120 --> 00:02:35.920
These 3 dialects have a relatively low degree 
of mutual intelligibility.

00:02:35.920 --> 00:02:40.500
So some people consider them to be distinct languages 
and not just dialects of one language.

00:02:40.760 --> 00:02:46.440
The differences between these dialects have been 
compounded by the fact that the Kurds are spread out 
across several different countries

00:02:46.480 --> 00:02:52.340
and have no central media or school system 
and haven't had much interaction with
the other areas of Kurdistan.

00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:56.280
And there has also been some influence from the
official languages of the countries where they live.

00:02:56.320 --> 00:03:01.600
Kurmanji is written in the Latin alphabet like Turkish, 
while Sorani is written in a Perso-Arabic script.

00:03:01.820 --> 00:03:03.700
Pehlewani is written in either script.

00:03:04.000 --> 00:03:08.340
On top of these main Kurdish dialects, or Kurdish languages depending on how you look at it,

00:03:08.560 --> 00:03:11.060
there are also some other languages
spoken by some Kurds

00:03:11.260 --> 00:03:18.220
A few million Kurds speak either Zaza or Gorani,
which form a separate sub branch of 
the northwestern Iranian languages.

00:03:18.580 --> 00:03:22.820
They're still quite closely related to the Kurdish dialects 
and since they are spoken by Kurds,

00:03:22.820 --> 00:03:27.780
some people consider them to be Kurdish dialects,
for reasons related to cultural and ethnic identity.

00:03:27.780 --> 00:03:29.320
Linguistic freedom.

00:03:29.660 --> 00:03:33.400
A big part of the story of Kurdish is
the desire for linguistic freedom.

00:03:33.680 --> 00:03:39.420
In Turkey the Kurdish language was heavily suppressed 
for decades, it was illegal to print Kurdish language materials.

00:03:39.480 --> 00:03:44.920
It was a prohibited language in government institutions 
and schools and, in public, it was prohibited to speak it.

00:03:45.180 --> 00:03:48.420
Even though not everyone followed that rule in public, 
that was the rule.

00:03:48.600 --> 00:03:56.200
These days, those policies have been relaxed but 
Kurds still want certain freedoms like the right to use 
the Kurdish language as the language of instruction
in regional schools.

00:03:56.460 --> 00:04:01.420
There was a similar situation in Syria and Iraq, where 
the language was made illegal and heavily suppressed.

00:04:01.560 --> 00:04:05.060
From what I understand, Iran has not aggressively 
suppress the Kurdish language

00:04:05.060 --> 00:04:09.380
but they have discouraged its use and
promoted assimilation  to the Persian language.

00:04:09.560 --> 00:04:13.440
There is one country where Kurdish 
is an official language and that is Iraq.

00:04:13.580 --> 00:04:20.080
And there is one area where Kurds have official 
autonomy and that's in Iraqi Kurdistan which is run 
by the kurdish regional government.

00:04:20.180 --> 00:04:24.400
There, Kurdish is the language of instruction in schools 
and it's used in the media.

00:04:25.460 --> 00:04:32.000
Since there are speakers of both Kurmanji and Sorani 
dialects in Iraqi Kurdistan, the government promotes 
both of them in schools and in the media.

00:04:32.120 --> 00:04:36.880
There was now also another de facto autonomous 
Kurdish region and that is in Syrian Kurdistan.

00:04:36.880 --> 00:04:38.420
It is known as Rojava.

00:04:38.560 --> 00:04:45.140
Due to the Civil War, the Syrian government is no longer 
in control of the area and all restrictions
on the Kurdish language are now irrelevant.

00:04:45.340 --> 00:04:48.480
Schools have introduced a new Kurdish curriculum, 
taught in Kurdish.

00:04:49.620 --> 00:04:52.760
The form of Kurdish used in Syrian Kurdistan
is Kurmanji.

00:04:52.980 --> 00:04:58.000
With Kurdish autonomy and Kurdish language schools 
now existing in both Iraq and in Syria,

00:04:58.100 --> 00:05:01.580
the Kurdish language might become stronger 
and more standardized in the future.

00:05:01.660 --> 00:05:03.340
So what exactly is Kurdish like?

00:05:03.440 --> 00:05:06.040
Well, that depends on what variety of Kurdish
you're talking about.

00:05:06.080 --> 00:05:09.440
So let's take a look at both, Kurmanji 
and after that we'll look at Sorani.

00:05:10.060 --> 00:05:12.160
Here are a few interesting features of Kurmanji.

00:05:12.340 --> 00:05:14.820
Kurmanji nouns are all either masculine or feminine.

00:05:15.040 --> 00:05:16.600
And there are four noun cases.

00:05:16.720 --> 00:05:20.640
It is generally an SOV language,
though certain sentences are SVO.

00:05:20.720 --> 00:05:22.820
Kurmanji has something called ergativity.

00:05:22.940 --> 00:05:27.840
Kurmanji has prepositions and postpositions 
and it also has something called circumpositions.

00:05:27.980 --> 00:05:30.280
Let's have a look at the nouns and their cases first.

00:05:30.280 --> 00:05:34.200
Kurmanji has four noun cases: 
nominative, oblique, construct and vocative.

00:05:34.320 --> 00:05:38.000
The case endings are different, depending
on the gender and number of the noun.

00:05:38.220 --> 00:05:40.440
Here's an example with the word for "man"

00:05:53.880 --> 00:05:56.300
Now here's an example using the word for "woman"

00:06:09.700 --> 00:06:13.280
You may have noticed that the plural case endings 
are the same for both genders.

00:06:13.300 --> 00:06:17.180
Because in Kurmanji, gender applies only
to singular nouns, not the plural nouns.

00:06:17.320 --> 00:06:20.160
Okay, let's look at some example sentences, 
including Kurmanji.

00:06:20.360 --> 00:06:22.880
Here we'll see that Kurmanji is generally SOV.

00:06:23.620 --> 00:06:25.140
This means "I see the men"

00:06:28.220 --> 00:06:32.480
So if we translate this sentence directly, it's like: 
"I - man - see"

00:06:32.660 --> 00:06:35.780
"Ez" equals "I" , "mirov" equals "man"

00:06:36.360 --> 00:06:41.000
"an" is the oblique case marker and also
 the plural marker. And "dibînim" is "see"

00:06:41.220 --> 00:06:45.440
So you can see that the sentence is SOV,
with the object coming in the middle position.

00:06:48.600 --> 00:06:51.800
This means "We are Kurdish" or "We are Kurds".

00:06:52.080 --> 00:06:55.900
If we try to translate this sentence directly,
it's like "We - Kurds - are"

00:06:56.080 --> 00:07:01.280
"Em" equals "we", "kurd" equals "Kurd". 
In equals "are", that's the present tense copula.

00:07:01.500 --> 00:07:03.420
Again we can see that this is SOV.

00:07:03.640 --> 00:07:09.320
This sentence doesn't actually have an object 
but it has something called a compliment,
which comes in the same position as the object.

00:07:09.640 --> 00:07:14.800
Now, why does the noun in the first sentence have 
an ending on it marked in yellow 
but in the second sentence there isn't one?

00:07:14.940 --> 00:07:19.140
That's because nominative case has no ending 
in either singular or plural in Kurmanji.

00:07:19.380 --> 00:07:21.740
So we only know, it's plural from the context.

00:07:21.920 --> 00:07:25.420
And here the context is that the subject is "we".
So we know it's plural.

00:07:26.740 --> 00:07:29.180
Kurmanji exhibit something called ergativity.

00:07:29.200 --> 00:07:34.320
In ergative languages, the subject of a verb sometimes 
functions as though it's the object of the sentence.

00:07:34.520 --> 00:07:38.040
In Kurmanji, this happens when the verb 
is in one of the past tenses.

00:07:38.340 --> 00:07:42.980
The agent, that is the "doer" of the action,
is in the oblique case like an object.

00:07:43.300 --> 00:07:48.340
And the patient, that is the receiver of the action, 
is in the nominative case, like a subject.

00:07:49.000 --> 00:07:51.680
The verb then takes an ending to agree with the patient.

00:07:54.600 --> 00:07:56.700
We would translate this as "I saw him"

00:07:56.960 --> 00:07:59.580
But it's more like "By me he was seen".

00:07:59.840 --> 00:08:02.340
Literally word for word it's "me he saw"

00:08:02.460 --> 00:08:06.340
But the verb ending confirms that 
he is actually the receiver of the action.

00:08:08.840 --> 00:08:12.280
This means "He saw me" or "By him, I was seen"

00:08:12.580 --> 00:08:14.620
Word by word it's : "him I saw"

00:08:14.760 --> 00:08:18.700
But again, the verb ending confirms 
that "I" is the receiver of the action.

00:08:18.880 --> 00:08:23.540
So here "im" is the ending for the first person singular 
and that matches the patient of the sentence.

00:08:23.540 --> 00:08:24.960
And that's "I" in this case.

00:08:25.120 --> 00:08:30.480
I find ergativity quite difficult to wrap my head around. 
It's not found in most Indo-European languages.

00:08:30.480 --> 00:08:35.040
It's found in Kurdish, it's found in Pashto and it's found 
in some of the northern Indian languages.

00:08:35.220 --> 00:08:40.260
Okay, if we want to make that last sentence negative, 
we simply add a negative prefix to the verb.

00:08:43.540 --> 00:08:48.600
This means "he didn't see me". This is an example 
of how Kurmanji uses lots of different affixes.

00:08:49.800 --> 00:08:56.460
Kurmanji has prepositions which come before nouns,  
phrases or pronouns and post positions which come 
after nouns, phrases or pronouns.

00:08:56.660 --> 00:09:00.340
But it also has circumpositions 
which wrap around the noun or pronoun.

00:09:00.420 --> 00:09:02.760
Or the entire noun phrase. For example:

00:09:04.620 --> 00:09:05.880
this means "in front of"

00:09:09.560 --> 00:09:12.140
This means "there's a tree in front of our house".

00:09:12.200 --> 00:09:18.840
So if we try to read the sentence word by word, it's: 
"in front - house - our - of - tree - there is"

00:09:19.080 --> 00:09:26.460
Now, I don't know if "of" is the part that I should have 
separated but the point there is that in "front of" is split 
into two pieces and surrounds the noun phrase.

00:09:27.600 --> 00:09:32.080
Now, let's look at some of those Kurmanji sentences again 
and we'll compare them to some Sorani sentences.

00:09:32.820 --> 00:09:35.300
Again here are the sentences for "I see the men",

00:09:35.540 --> 00:09:36.640
in Kurmanji:

00:09:39.460 --> 00:09:40.380
in Sorani:

00:09:42.660 --> 00:09:47.840
Now one difference that you can see is that 
the preposition is different in the two different languages.

00:09:47.860 --> 00:09:52.540
So, in Kurmanji, it's "Ez" that means "I" 
but, in Sorani, it's "min".

00:09:52.900 --> 00:09:53.720
Why is it "min"?

00:09:53.880 --> 00:10:01.400
Well, in Kurmanji "min" is the 1st person singular pronoun 
in the oblique case, like an object pronoun,
while "Ez" is the nominative case.

00:10:01.700 --> 00:10:06.260
But in Sorani, the pronouns have no cases.
So there's just one word and that word is "min".

00:10:07.180 --> 00:10:11.300
Another different word here is "piyawekan", 
which is the word for "man" in Sorani.

00:10:11.320 --> 00:10:13.560
That's different from the Kurmanji word "mirovan"

00:10:13.620 --> 00:10:16.640
I think this is just an alternate word for "man" in Sorani

00:10:16.640 --> 00:10:19.880
I think in Sorani the word "mirov" means "man" 
more as "a human".

00:10:19.920 --> 00:10:24.500
Now both words for men here have the ending "an"
but it's actually different in both languages.

00:10:24.500 --> 00:10:29.240
In Kurmanji, its' the oblique case plural marker 
but in Sorani, there are no cases.

00:10:29.240 --> 00:10:32.020
So, in Sorani, it's just the general plural marker.

00:10:33.240 --> 00:10:35.420
Now the sentences for "We are Kurds"

00:10:35.560 --> 00:10:36.440
in Kurmanji

00:10:38.580 --> 00:10:39.560
and in Sorani:

00:10:41.060 --> 00:10:44.240
Now here again, we can see that the pronoun 
is different in the two languages.

00:10:44.240 --> 00:10:49.480
It's the first person plural pronoun
but, in Kurmanji, it's "em" and in Sorani, it's "êma"

00:10:49.520 --> 00:10:52.520
And, in both languages, we have 
the present tense copula "in".

00:10:52.680 --> 00:10:57.080
The only difference here is the orthography.
In Kurmanji, there's a space before the copula.

00:10:57.280 --> 00:10:59.280
Now the sentences for "I saw him",

00:10:59.480 --> 00:11:00.440
in Kurmanji:

00:11:02.780 --> 00:11:03.760
and in Sorani:

00:11:05.220 --> 00:11:11.280
These sentences look quite similar. The Sorani sentence 
has an extra "m" attached to the object of the sentence.

00:11:11.460 --> 00:11:14.800
That "m" is actually an affix representing the subject "min".

00:11:15.300 --> 00:11:19.920
In a Sorani past tense sentence, the subject is 
represented as an affix before the verb.

00:11:20.140 --> 00:11:22.900
Here the subject is also stated overtly as "min".

00:11:23.020 --> 00:11:26.960
But the sentence is possible without that first word. 
In Kurmanji, it's not like that.

00:11:27.780 --> 00:11:30.040
Now the sentences for "he saw me",

00:11:30.040 --> 00:11:31.160
in Kurmanji:

00:11:33.720 --> 00:11:34.860
and in Sorani:

00:11:36.380 --> 00:11:38.420
This example is similar to the last one.

00:11:38.580 --> 00:11:43.220
We can see that the pronouns are different in Sorani 
because Sorani has no case distinction.

00:11:43.380 --> 00:11:47.640
And we can see that, in Kurmanji, the verb has an ending 
that the Sorani verb doesn't have.

00:11:48.120 --> 00:11:51.860
In the Kurmanji sentence, an object's suffix 
is attached to the end of the verb.

00:11:52.040 --> 00:11:55.740
In Sorani, on the other hand, 
a subject affix is added before the verb.

00:11:56.920 --> 00:11:59.320
And here are the sentences for "He didn't see me",

00:11:59.320 --> 00:12:00.280
in Kurmanji:

00:12:02.980 --> 00:12:03.980
in Sorani:

00:12:05.680 --> 00:12:09.060
Now, this sentence is basically the same
as the last one, except that it's negative.

00:12:09.060 --> 00:12:12.840
And we can see that Kurmanji and Sorani
use negation in the same way.

00:12:13.820 --> 00:12:16.780
Here are the sentences for 
"There's a tree in front of our house",

00:12:16.900 --> 00:12:18.000
in Kurmanji:

00:12:21.700 --> 00:12:22.600
in Sorani:

00:12:25.520 --> 00:12:29.660
In the Kurmanji sentence, we have a circumposition
 that wraps around the noun phrase.

00:12:29.820 --> 00:12:33.660
But, in the Sorani sentence, there is a preposition 
that comes before the noun phrase.

00:12:33.960 --> 00:12:35.720
And the preposition is totally different.

00:12:36.060 --> 00:12:38.340
And the noun phrase itself is also somewhat different.

00:12:38.460 --> 00:12:42.260
"mala" means "house" in Kurmanji, 
while "mall" means "house" in Sorani.

00:12:42.520 --> 00:12:46.020
And "me" means "our" in Kurmanji 
while in Sorani it's "man".

00:12:46.540 --> 00:12:50.900
The "ek" after "mall" is an indefinite article
which doesn't exist in Kurmanji.

00:12:51.020 --> 00:12:54.720
And the "e" is a connector which also 
isn't used in the Kurmanji sentence

00:12:55.740 --> 00:12:59.780
So, as you can see, there are some significant 
differences between Kurmanji and Sorani.

00:13:00.120 --> 00:13:01.900
So, which variety of Kurdish should you learn?

00:13:01.940 --> 00:13:05.440
Well, if you're planning on spending some time 
in the Kurdistan Region, then it would make sense

00:13:05.440 --> 00:13:08.680
to learn the variety of the place where you plan 
to spend the most time.

00:13:08.960 --> 00:13:12.980
And, in the future, you can always learn to adapt 
and speak a different dialect by extension.

00:13:12.980 --> 00:13:14.900
Is Kurdish a difficult language to learn?

00:13:15.020 --> 00:13:20.500
Well, there are some things like ergativity
that are probably challenging for people
who have never encountered them before.

00:13:20.660 --> 00:13:25.340
But, probably, some of the most challenging things 
will be the dialectal variation in the language.

00:13:25.340 --> 00:13:29.980
And the lack of a single standard language 
as well as a general lack of learning materials.

00:13:30.220 --> 00:13:36.180
On the other hand, from what I understand, 
if you have a previous knowledge of Persian, 
that will help you a great deal and learning Kurdish.

00:13:36.360 --> 00:13:42.340
And Kurdish people are also all very welcoming 
and friendly and very enthusiastic about
helping you to learn their language.

00:13:42.360 --> 00:13:44.840
And that kind of support is always very encouraging.

00:13:44.940 --> 00:13:47.520
Okay, the question of the day. For Kurdish speakers:

00:13:47.700 --> 00:13:51.680
How easy is it for you to communicate
with speakers of other varieties of Kurdish?

00:13:51.720 --> 00:13:56.500
And do you consider them all dialects of a single language, 
or do you consider them distinct languages?

00:13:56.760 --> 00:13:58.400
And for people who studied Kurdish:

00:13:58.480 --> 00:14:00.520
What did you find challenging about the language?

00:14:00.740 --> 00:14:05.420
And, in general, what was your experience with the language?
We'd like to hear about it in the comments down below.

00:14:05.660 --> 00:14:09.880
Definitely be sure to check out Langfocus
on Twitter, on Facebook and on Instagram.

00:14:09.960 --> 00:14:15.120
And again, I'd like to say thanks to all of my Patreon 
